  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nTonight through Sunday Night...\n\nWeather conditions across our CWA will remain quiet and dry through \ntonight thanks to surface ridging from the northeast. As we go \nthrough the overnight period and through early Sunday morning, the \nsfc ridging will gradually weaken. Mid to high cumulus cloud cover \nwill be possible across portions of north MS and east MS as clouds \npush across the area from west to east. Nighttime temperatures will \ndip into the low to mid 50s areawide. Although HREF fog guidance is \nbeginning to show low probabilities (less than 20%) of patchy fog \nmainly across portions of east MS and south MS, there is not enough \nsupport from the REFS to suggest widespread fog potential. Fog \ngraphics will not be introduced at this time given the low \nprobability of fog development highlighted by HREF dense fog \nguidance, however shallow patchy fog will be possible particularly \nafter dawn. \n\nA cold front will approach the area heading into Sunday. As the cold \nfront pushes into northern portions of our CWA Sunday afternoon, \nportions of the Delta will have a low chance of rain with PoPs \naround 20%. As we head into late Sunday afternoon/Sunday evening, \nthe cold front will continue to push east across our area. This \ncombined with southwest flow will help increase moisture across much \nof our area. Rain chances will range between 15-85% with the \ngreatest chances in the MS Delta and along and north of I-20. Rain \nchances will begin to gradually decrease heading into late Sunday \nnight with a few lingering showers possible south of I-20. Although \nrain chances will be present, rainfall amounts are expected to be \nlimited (0.25 in or less) and won't relive drought conditions.\nNighttime lows will dip into the upper 40s/lower 50s in the Delta \nregion. Elsewhere, overnight lows will dip into the mid to upper \n50s. /CR/\n\nMonday through Saturday... \n\nAs the cold front continues to push southeast across our CWA heading \ninto Monday, moisture will begin to increase across portions of \ncentral MS and southern MS. This will introduce low to medium \nchances (15-50%) of scattered convection mainly for areas along and \nsouth of I-20. Rain chances will come to an end late Monday night. \nPost-frontal passage will cool temperatures to below seasonal \naverages (5-10 degrees cooler) heading into the Thanksgiving \nholiday. A brief shortwave disturbance may bring an isolated (25% or \nless) shower or two in eastern MS on Wednesday but the Thanksgiving \nholiday and weekend is expected to be dry and quiet. /CR/",
